0px" width=3D"300" height=3D"300">
  Please use a browser that supports "canvas"
</canvas>
<br>
<div>Click and drag in the window to rotate the objects.</div>

<!-- Load the JavaScript libraries and data files for the WebGL rendering -=
->











<!--
  Create an instance of the learn_webgl class, and start the WebGL program.
  We do this here to pass the canvas ID into the javascript code.
-->


<a href=3D"https://learnwebgl.brown37.net/08_projections/perspective_exampl=
e/perspective_example.html" target=3D"_blank">Open this webgl program in a =
new tab or window</a></div>
<!-- end of webgl code demo --><p>Each step in the graphics pipeline is per=
forming a specific task but also
preparing data for follow-on steps. This is especially
true for the projection transform.</p>
<ul class=3D"simple">
<li>The major task of a projection transformation is to project a 3D scene =
onto a 2D screen.</li>
<li>A projection transformation also prepares for these follow-on tasks:<ul=
>
<li>Clipping - the removal of elements that are not in the camera=E2=80=99s=
 line of sight.</li>
<li>Viewport mapping - convert a camera=E2=80=99s <em>viewing window</em> i=
nto the pixels of an image.</li>
<li>Hidden surface removal - determining which objects are in front of othe=
r objects.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Before a projection operation a (<em>x</em>,<em>y</em>,<em>z</em>) verte=
x
represents a location in 3-dimensional space. After a projection the meanin=
g
of the values have changed:</p>
<ul class=3D"simple">
<li>The (<em>x</em>,<em>y</em>) values of a vertex represent its location o=
n a 2-dimensional screen.</li>
<li>The <em>z</em> value represents the distance of the vertex from the vir=
tual camera
and is used for <em>hidden surface</em> removal.</li>
<li>All 3 values, <em>x</em>, <em>y</em>, and <em>z</em>, are used for clip=
ping, which removes points,
lines, or triangles that are not visible to the scene=E2=80=99s virtual cam=
era.</li>
</ul>
<p>Before we jump into the details of <em>projections</em>,
we need to discuss the standard <em>clipping volume</em> that all scenes ar=
e
mapped into before they are rendered.</p>
<div class=3D"section" id=3D"the-clipping-volume">
<h2>The Clipping Volume<a class=3D"headerlink" href=3D"https://learnwebgl.b=
rown37.net/08_projections/projections_introduction.html#the-clipping-volume=
" title=3D"Permalink to this headline">=C2=B6</a></h2>
<div class=3D"figure align-right">
<a class=3D"reference internal image-reference" href=3D"https://learnwebgl.=
brown37.net/_images/clipping_volume.png"><img alt=3D"Clipping volume" src=
=3D"https://learnwebgl.brown37.net/_images/clipping_volume.png" style=3D"wi=
dth: 236px; height: 235px;"></a>
</div>
<p>When you point a real camera at something to take a picture, only a part
of the real-world is visible in the camera=E2=80=99s field-of-view. The sam=
e
is true of the human eye; you only see what is in front of you. We want
the same behavior for our virtual scenes. The process of determining what
is visible and what is not visible is called <em>clipping</em>. Clipping is
done automatically by WegGL. To make
clipping as efficient as possible, clipping is done using a cube centered
at the origin.</p>
<p>The <em>clipping volume</em> is defined in <em>Normalized Device Coordin=
ates</em> (NDC)
which is a 2 unit wide cube, with
the X axis going from -1 to +1, the Y axis going from -1 to +1, and the
Z axis going from -1 to +1, as shown in the image to the right.</p>
<p>Did you notice
that the clipping volume is defined in a left-handed coordinate system?
The Z-axis is pointing into the page. Wow! Previous lessons have
stated that WebGL always uses a right-handed coordinate system and now it
doesn=E2=80=99t. What is going on?</p>
<p>The short story is this: the hardware GPU that produces
the graphics is designed for speed and efficiency, while the WebGL API
is designed for programmers. Most people are <a class=3D"reference external=
" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Handedness">right handed</a>, so fo=
r most
people the right-hand rule feels natural and comfortable, while the
internal workings of the graphics hardware can typically be ignored. The
projection matrix is the last operation under programmer
control that manipulates the geometric
data of a scene. So the projection transformation can convert to a
left-handed coordinate system and not affect anything the programmer has
done with the geometric data in a right-handed coordinate system.</p>
<p>You can visually see the left-handed coordinate system of the GPU in the
following demo program. Observe what happens
when you include an projection matrix and when you don=E2=80=99t.</p>
<!-- webgl code demo start -->
<div id=3D"W3_webgl_demo" class=3D"webgl_demo_container">

<p>Render 3 triangles with or without a projection matrix.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The red triangle is at z =3D -0.25</li>
  <li>The green triangle is at z =3D 0.0</li>
  <li>The blue triangle is at z =3D +0.25</li>
</ul>

<!--The canvas window for rendering 3D graphics -->
<canvas id=3D"W3_canvas" class=3D"canvas3D" ,=3D"" style=3D"width:400px; he=
ight:400px" width=3D"400" height=3D"400">
  Please use a browser that supports "canvas"
</canvas>
<br>

<!-- An HTML control element that can be used to modify the rendering behav=
ior -->
<input id=3D"W3_use_projection" type=3D"checkbox" unchecked=3D"">Use a proj=
ection matrix for rendering<br>

<!-- Load the JavaScript libraries and data files for the WebGL rendering -=
->












<!--
  Create an instance of the learn_webgl class, and start the WebGL program.
  We do this here to pass the canvas ID into the javascript code.
-->


<a href=3D"https://learnwebgl.brown37.net/08_projections/viewing_volume/vie=
wing_volume.html" target=3D"_blank">Open this webgl program in a new tab or=
 window</a></div>
<!-- end of webgl code demo --><p>Given that the <em>clipping volume</em> u=
ses a left-handed coordinate system,
there are two very important implications to the graphics programmer:</p>
<ul class=3D"simple">
<li><strong>You must always include a projection matrix in your vertex tran=
sform</strong>, even
if you don=E2=80=99t think you need one. All of the transformations
we have defined previously assumed a right-handed coordinate system. If
you don=E2=80=99t include a projection matrix, all of the other transformat=
ion matrices
will not work correctly.</li>
<li>If you create your own projection matrix, it should negate the z values=
 of all vertices.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class=3D"section" id=3D"clipping">
<h2>Clipping<a class=3D"headerlink" href=3D"https://learnwebgl.brown37.net/=
08_projections/projections_introduction.html#clipping" title=3D"Permalink t=
o this headline">=C2=B6</a></h2>
<p>Consider how clipping points might be done using programming logic. To d=
etermine
if a point defined as (x,y,z) is inside the clipping volume, you could writ=
e
code like this:</p>
<div class=3D"highlight-JavaScript"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre><span cla=
ss=3D"k">if</span> <span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"nx">x</span> <s=
pan class=3D"o">&gt;=3D</span> <span class=3D"o">-</span><span class=3D"mi"=
>1</span> <span class=3D"nx">and</span> <span class=3D"nx">x</span> <span c=
lass=3D"o">&lt;=3D</span> <span class=3D"mi">1</span> <span class=3D"nx">an=
d</span>
    <span class=3D"nx">y</span> <span class=3D"o">&gt;=3D</span> <span clas=
s=3D"o">-</span><span class=3D"mi">1</span> <span class=3D"nx">and</span> <=
span class=3D"nx">y</span> <span class=3D"o">&lt;=3D</span> <span class=3D"=
mi">1</span> <span class=3D"nx">and</span>
    <span class=3D"nx">z</span> <span class=3D"o">&gt;=3D</span> <span clas=
s=3D"o">-</span><span class=3D"mi">1</span> <span class=3D"nx">and</span> <=
span class=3D"nx">z</span> <span class=3D"o">&lt;=3D</span> <span class=3D"=
mi">1</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
  <span class=3D"nx">point_is_visible</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <=
span class=3D"kc">true</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"k">else</span>
  <span class=3D"nx">point_is_visible</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <=
span class=3D"kc">false</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
</pre></div>
</div>
<p>However, since the clipping volume is uniform and symmetrical about the =
origin,
we can simplify this code using an <em>absolute value</em> function:</p>
<div class=3D"highlight-JavaScript"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre><span cla=
ss=3D"k">if</span> <span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"nx">abs</span><=
span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"nx">x</span><span class=3D"p">)</sp=
an> <span class=3D"o">&lt;=3D</span> <span class=3D"mi">1</span> <span clas=
s=3D"nx">and</span> <span class=3D"nx">abs</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>=
<span class=3D"nx">y</span><span class=3D"p">)</span> <span class=3D"o">&lt=
;=3D</span> <span class=3D"mi">1</span> <span class=3D"nx">and</span> <span=
 class=3D"nx">abs</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"nx">z</spa=
n><span class=3D"p">)</span> <span class=3D"o">&lt;=3D</span> <span class=
=3D"mi">1</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
     <span class=3D"nx">point_is_visible</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span=
> <span class=3D"kc">true</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"k">else</span>
     <span class=3D"nx">point_is_visible</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span=
> <span class=3D"kc">false</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
</pre></div>
</div>
<p>Furthermore, you could set the boolean value with just a simple assignme=
nt like this:</p>
<div class=3D"highlight-JavaScript"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre><span cla=
ss=3D"nx">point_is_visible</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=
=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"nx">abs</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span=
 class=3D"nx">x</span><span class=3D"p">)</span> <span class=3D"o">&lt;=3D<=
/span> <span class=3D"mi">1</span> <span class=3D"nx">and</span> <span clas=
s=3D"nx">abs</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"nx">y</span><sp=
an class=3D"p">)</span> <span class=3D"o">&lt;=3D</span> <span class=3D"mi"=
>1</span> <span class=3D"nx">and</span> <span class=3D"nx">abs</span><span =
class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"nx">z</span><span class=3D"p">)</span> <=
span class=3D"o">&lt;=3D</span> <span class=3D"mi">1</span><span class=3D"p=
">)</span>
</pre></div>
</div>
<p>Actually clipping is done in homogeneous coordinates (which you will lea=
rn more
about in a couple of lessons). For a (x,y,z,w) vertex, the clipping test wo=
uld
be</p>
<div class=3D"highlight-JavaScript"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre><span cla=
ss=3D"nx">point_is_visible</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=
=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"nx">abs</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span=
 class=3D"nx">x</span><span class=3D"p">)</span> <span class=3D"o">&lt;=3D<=
/span> <span class=3D"nx">w</span> <span class=3D"nx">and</span> <span clas=
s=3D"nx">abs</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"nx">y</span><sp=
an class=3D"p">)</span> <span class=3D"o">&lt;=3D</span> <span class=3D"nx"=
>w</span> <span class=3D"nx">and</span> <span class=3D"nx">abs</span><span =
class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"nx">z</span><span class=3D"p">)</span> <=
span class=3D"o">&lt;=3D</span> <span class=3D"nx">w</span><span class=3D"p=
">)</span>
</pre></div>
</div>
<div class=3D"sidebar">
<p class=3D"first sidebar-title">Clipping Algorithms</p>
<p class=3D"last">If you really want to understand why WebGL only renders p=
oints, lines, and
triangles, study <a class=3D"reference external" href=3D"http://gamedev.sta=
ckexchange.com/questions/8326/triangle-rectangle-intersection-in-2d">clippi=
ng</a>! Clipping is a complex problem
even for simple triangles.</p>
</div>
<p>But the details don=E2=80=99t matter to most programmers. What does matt=
er is that
the symmetrical design of the <em>clipping volume</em> facilitates fast cli=
pping.
It is helpful to remember that WebGL is really
a hardware specification with a software API (application programmer interf=
ace).
WebGL=E2=80=99s design is optimized for hardware implementation.</p>
</div>
<div class=3D"section" id=3D"when-does-a-projection-need-to-happen">
<h2>When Does a Projection Need To Happen?<a class=3D"headerlink" href=3D"h=
ttps://learnwebgl.brown37.net/08_projections/projections_introduction.html#=
when-does-a-projection-need-to-happen" title=3D"Permalink to this headline"=
>=C2=B6</a></h2>
<p>A projection transformation must happen after a scene has been moved in
front of a virtual camera, but before clipping occurs. A projection operati=
on
can be performed using a 4-by-4 matrix multiplication, so it is typically
combined with the <em>model</em> transform and the <em>camera</em> (or <em>=
view</em>) transform
that have been conceptually performed in previous stages of the pipeline.
In reality, the <em>model</em>, <em>view</em>, and <em>projection</em>
transformations are typically performed with a single matrix multiplication=
.
In your JavaScript code you will typically create a single
transformation matrix and pass it to your <em>vertex shader</em>.</p>
<!-- matrixeq start -->
<div id=3D"M8445" class=3D"matrixeq_container">
<span class=3D"operator" style=3D"line-height:65%">VertexTransform =3D</spa=
n><span id=3D"M8445_1" class=3D"matrixtable"><span class=3D"vector"><span>P=
rojectionMatrix<br></span></span></span><span class=3D"operator" style=3D"l=
ine-height:230%">*</span><span id=3D"M8445_3" class=3D"matrixtable"><span c=
lass=3D"vector"><span>ViewMatrix<br></span></span></span><span class=3D"ope=
rator" style=3D"line-height:230%">*</span><span id=3D"M8445_5" class=3D"mat=
rixtable"><span class=3D"vector"><span>ModelMatrix<br></span></span></span>=
<span class=3D"label" style=3D"line-height:230%"> Eq1</span></div>
<!-- end of matrixeq --><p>The following lessons describe how to build a 4-=
by-4 projection matrix.</p>
</div>
<div class=3D"section" id=3D"glossary">
<h2>Glossary<a class=3D"headerlink" href=3D"https://learnwebgl.brown37.net/=
08_projections/projections_introduction.html#glossary" title=3D"Permalink t=
o this headline">=C2=B6</a></h2>
<dl class=3D"glossary docutils">
<dt id=3D"term-projection">projection</dt>
<dd>Transform the vertices of a 3D model into 2D points on a 2D <em>viewing=
 window</em>.</dd>
<dt id=3D"term-viewing-window">viewing window</dt>
<dd>A rectangular 2D region on which a 3D world is projected.</dd>
<dt id=3D"term-orthogonal-projection">orthogonal projection</dt>
<dd>Project all vertices of a 3D model along straight lines parallel to the=
 Z axis.</dd>
<dt id=3D"term-perspective-projection">perspective projection</dt>
<dd>Project all vertices of a 3D model along vectors to the camera=E2=80=99=
s location.
Where the vector hits the 2D <em>viewing window</em> becomes it=E2=80=99s r=
endered location.</dd>
<dt id=3D"term-clipping">clipping</dt>
<dd>The process of determining what is visible and not visible in a virtual
camera=E2=80=99s field of view.</dd>
<dt id=3D"term-normalized-device-coordinates-ndc">Normalized Device Coordin=
ates (NDC)</dt>
<dd>The 3D coordinate system that all scenes are converted into before clip=
ping is performed.</dd>
</dl>
</div>
</div>


